\section{Introduction}
Toto (ISO 639-3 txo, Glottocode toto1302) is a Sino-Tibetan language spoken mainly in Totopara, West Bengal, India, near the Bhutan border. It was traditionally oral and has been written in Bengali script. A Toto alphabet was devised by Dhaniram Toto, who received the Padma Shri for this work.
\section{Primary Educational Resources}
Volunteers are reported to teach the Toto script to local children, but no published primers or textbooks were found in search results. A news article on learning the script:
\begin{itemize}
\item \url{https://villagesquare.in/learning-the-abc-of-toto-language/}
\end{itemize}
\section{Linguistic and Reference Materials}
\begin{itemize}
\item Wikipedia, Toto language: \url{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Toto_language}
\item Omniglot, Toto writing: \url{https://omniglot.com/writing/toto.htm}
\item Linguistics paper (Academia Sinica) on Toto: \url{https://www.ling.sinica.edu.tw/upload/researcher_manager_result/f822204da4a29c59b07f0a38aa990354.pdf}
\end{itemize}
Search summaries also mention a first Toto dictionary (about 1,200 entries with English and Bengali, by Bhakta Toto, 2023) and the Linguistic Survey of India chapter by P. Perumalsamy. No direct URL for either appeared in the results.
\section{Religious and Community Resources}
None found in search results.
\section{Digital and Audio Resources}
\begin{itemize}
\item arXiv paper on a trilingual Toto-Bangla-English learning application: \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22629v1}
\end{itemize}
\section{Curriculum Development Context}
Press coverage describes community efforts to protect and teach the language.
